 "findings": {
  "low_level": [
   "Registers and spills: at 512 threads (128-register cap) N6b holds 128 registers with 0 spill at N 16384 (IPT 32) and 128 with 3852 to 3856 bytes of spill, 424 bytes of stack, 229 to 230 STL and 733 to 735 LDL at N 32768 (IPT 64); at 256 threads 255 registers with 0 spill at N 16384 (IPT 64) and 255 with 7276 bytes of spill, 632 bytes of stack, 330 STL and 1489 LDL at N 32768 (IPT 128). Halving the tile per thread halves the spill but does not remove it: the per-item image plus the per-bucket tracking state fill 128 registers at 64 items per thread as they filled 255 at 128.",
   "The control B1_idx at 512 threads: 64 registers at N 16384 and 100 to 103 at N 32768, 0 spill (40 bytes for fp32 N 16384), against 128 and 248 to 250 at 256 threads. SASS sizes: N6b 15216 to 8384 instructions (bf16 N 16384 latency kernel, 256 to 512 threads), 32208 to 17088 (N 32768); B1_idx 5344 to 3056 and 10296 to 5592.",
   "Occupancy in the throughput regime: N6b 1 block per SM at both block sizes and both N (255 and 128 registers), so the 512-thread form runs 16 warps per SM against 8; B1_idx 2 blocks per SM at N 16384 (both sizes) and 1 at N 32768."
  ],
  "algorithmic": [
   "N6b at 512 against 256 threads, latency (ratio 256 over 512): bf16 N 16384 1.05x, 1.09x, 1.19x at k 16, 64, 256 (5838, 7404, 9938 against 6102, 8099, 11837); bf16 N 32768 1.08x, 1.03x, 1.02x (18529, 25108, 27692 against 20035, 25759, 28207); fp32 N 16384 0.96x, 1.06x, 1.16x (6002, 7294, 9725 against 5752, 7761, 11273); fp32 N 32768 0.99x, 0.91x, 0.88x (14862, 20392, 24661 against 14661, 18586, 21773). Throughput: 1.14x, 1.19x, 1.24x at bf16 N 16384, 1.21x, 1.08x, 1.05x at bf16 N 32768, 1.09x, 1.15x, 1.24x at fp32 N 16384, 1.15x, 1.03x, 0.98x at fp32 N 32768. The 512-thread form helps N6b where it does not spill (N 16384, up to 1.19x latency and 1.24x throughput) and does little or harm at N 32768, where the remaining 3.9 KB of spill at 128 registers is a larger share of a 64-item sweep than 7.3 KB was of a 128-item sweep.",
   "Against B1_idx at the same block size, latency ratio B1_idx over N6b: at 256 threads 1.48x, 1.16x, 0.93x (bf16 N 16384, k 16, 64, 256), 0.84x, 0.66x, 0.66x (bf16 N 32768), 1.58x, 1.35x, 1.17x (fp32 N 16384), 1.06x, 1.03x, 1.00x (fp32 N 32768); at 512 threads 1.30x, 1.07x, 0.90x, then 0.73x, 0.55x, 0.53x, then 1.26x, 1.17x, 1.06x, then 0.79x, 0.69x, 0.64x. N6b's N 16384 k 16 cells are new (the N6 cards had N 1024, 4096 and 32768): 1.48x and 1.58x over B1_idx at 256 threads, the largest N6 margin measured so far, kept at 1.30x and 1.26x when both run at 512 threads. At N 32768 the 512-thread B1_idx (13507 bf16, 11800 fp32 at k 16) is 1.27x to 1.9x ahead of every N6b form, so the block size settles the N 32768 question for B1_idx and not for N6.",
   "Block size recommendation for N6b: 512 threads at N 16384 (both regimes, both types, 1.05x to 1.24x), 256 threads at fp32 N 32768 (the 512-thread form is 0.88x to 0.99x in latency), either at bf16 N 32768 (within 8 percent, both behind B1_idx by 1.4x to 1.9x). The N 32768 register problem of N6 (B1 report section 8) is not solved by the block size, it needs the shared-memory resident rest or the recompute of the bf16 image that the report lists."
  ],
  "negative": [
   "512 threads do not remove N6b's spill at N 32768 (3.9 KB against 7.3 KB), and fp32 N 32768 gets slower (0.88x to 0.99x): the 128-register cap at 16 warps leaves less per thread for the image and the trackers than 255 did at 8 warps, and the extra spill traffic of 16 warps competes for the same L1.",
   "N6b at 512 threads loses throughput to B1_idx at 512 threads at every cell (0.58x to 0.92x), where B1_idx's own gain from the block size is larger (1.27x to 1.44x at N 32768)."
  ],
  "surprises": [
   "The k 16 cells at N 16384 show the largest N6 advantage of the study (1.48x bf16, 1.58x fp32 over B1_idx at 256 threads), between the N 4096 figures (1.18x and 1.23x in the verified card) and the N 32768 losses; one bracket pass plus a finish against two or three radix passes pays most where the sweep is long but the registers still hold the tile.",
   "fp32 N 32768 is the one shape where 512 threads make N6b slower although the spill halves: the sweep per pass halves too, so the spill loads per sweep item stay, and the 16-warp block pays a 44-cycle barrier (T11) per pass boundary."
  ],
  "open_questions": [
   "N6b at 1024 threads (IPT 32 at N 32768, 64-register cap) was not measured; B1_idx and B3 at 1024 threads are in the N8 block-size batch.",
   "Whether N6b's N 16384 k 16 margin (1.48x and 1.58x) survives the throughput regime at a matched block size: at 256 threads N6b is 0.86x to 0.87x of B1_idx in throughput (1 block per SM against 2), at 512 threads 0.91x to 0.92x."
  ]
